Quoting  Janis Papanagnou  <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com>:
>...but, OTOH, my games vary a lot; in some games Rodney re-appeared
>just twice (or so) and in other games he appears 15 (or more) times.
>In some games I am affected by the force 40 times (even as chaotic)
>and I had played a lawful character that was thrown back just twice.
>Considering all that randomness, speedy play is, while important, not
>that significant a factor compared to the above mentioned;

The mysterious force is out of your control; however, either you are
descending levels or otherwise exposing yourself to more mysterious force
than you have to, or your memory is seriously at fault. (See below.)

Rodney's appearances are governed by chance, but the chance rolls around
on a regular schedule, so speedy play certainly reduces the chance of
seeing him several times.

There is a lot of randomness, but fundamentally delay post-Rodney is bad
because it drains your limited resources; hence I don't actually feel that
clearing the Sanctum is bad if you are prepared for any nuisance Rodney
might cause by turning up, because the graveyard is a rich source of
resources that will more than replenish what you use up on post-Rodney
effects.

I've just rolled a quick Perl script to simulate a character rising up
through Gehennom. You can't be mysterious-forced on the bottom 3 levels
(because that might nerf a Lawful clean out of the world); you can't be
mysterious-forced when leaving the Valley:

    if (Inhell && up && u.uhave.amulet && !newdungeon && !portal &&
                            (dunlev(&u.uz) < dunlevs_in_dungeon(&u.uz)-3)) {

so really Gehennom consists of 16-20 levels for our purposes.

After a million simulated runs, the numbers are as follows (for example,
20:0.5% shows that in 0.5% of games, at least 20 mysterious-forcings
appeared; 32:22 shows that in 22 games, at least 32 mysterious-forcings
appeared. Percentages are rounded to the nearest 0.1% and shown where they
_are_ at least 0.1%):

2:97.1% 4:84.1% 8:40.6% 12:12.3% 16:2.8% 20:0.5% 24:0.1% 28:135 32:23 36:3 40:0

Not one 40-force game. Even a 36-force game is vanishingly unlikely, and
you have to get down into the 24-force zone before a really keen player
might seriously expect to have it happen in years of play.

If you were Lawful, the numbers are more like this:

2:97.1% 4:85.5% 8:46.3% 12:17.8% 16:5.7% 20:1.6% 24:0.4% 28:0.1% 32:281
36:80 40:22

So it is quite plausible for Lawful characters to be mysterious-forced
twice. You might be wondering why the numbers are so similar for low
numbers of forces; it may be obvious when you consider that the chance for
_no_ mysterious force is independent of alignment.

Perhaps you are counting stairs climbed? For a Chaotic, the total number
of stairs climbed exiting Gehennom looks like this:

24:96.1% 28:78.9% 32:52.4% 36:28.9% 40:13.8% 44:5.9% 48:2.3% 52:0.9%
56:0.3% 60:0.1%

... so climbing 40 stairs is eminently plausible.

I reproduce the script below if anyone is interested. It's a horrible
hack.

#!/usr/bin/perl -w

$align=pop;$lawful=($align =~ /l/);$climb=($align=~ /c/);$attempts=0;
while ($attempts<1000000) {
  $maxlevel=16+int(rand(5));
  $attempts++; 
  if ($attempts ==1) {
    foreach $j (0..40) {
      $bad[$j]=0;
    }
  }
  $posn=$maxlevel;$force=0;$climbs=4;
  while ($posn) {
    $posn--; $climbs++;
    $i=int (rand($lawful ? 16 : 8)); 
    $posn++ if $i <2; $posn++ if $i <1;
    $posn+=2 if $i >14; $posn++ if $i>13;
    if ($posn>$maxlevel) {
      $climbs+=$posn-$maxlevel;$posn=$maxlevel;
    } 
    $force++ if $i <2; $force++ if $i>13;
  } 
  foreach $j (0..($climb ? $climbs : $force)) {
    $bad[$j]++;
  }
  next if $attempts %1000;
  foreach $k (0..10) {
    $j=$k*4;$j=2 if $k==0;
    $chance=(int(0.5+1000*$bad[$j]/$attempts))/10;
    $chance[$j]=($chance>0)? "$chance%" : "$bad[$j]";
    print "$j:$chance[$j] ";
  } 
  print " $attempts\n";
}
